# Writing a generic class

## Declare the parameter after the name

```java
public class Box<T> {
    private T value;
    public T get()           { return value; }
    public void set(T value) { this.value = value; }
}

Box<String> b = new Box<>("hi");
b.set(42);   // COMPILE ERROR — T is String
```

Written once, works for any type, each use checked.

## Multiple parameters

```java
public record Pair<A, B>(A first, B second) {}   // records are generic too
```

Cleanest way to return two values.

## Three "you can't" rules

- **No `new T()`** — pass a `Supplier<T>` / factory
- **No `static` field of type `T`** — `T` belongs to an instance
- **No `new T[]`** — back it with `Object[]` (§09)

## First taste of bounds

```java
class SortedBox<T extends Comparable<T>> { }   // now T has compareTo
```

`extends` = a **constraint** (§05), not inheritance.
